// The civil calendar -- the TYPES and the VALIDATING CONSTRUCTORS (cycle
// 0.1.0), HINNANT'S TWO ALGORITHMS (cycle 0.1.1), and the DERIVED FIELDS --
// the weekday of a date, its ordinal date and its ISO week date, with the two
// constructors that build a date back from them (cycle 0.1.3). Governed by
// `meta/specs/CALENDAR.md`; this file implements §3 and C-6 through C-15.
//
// THE PUBLIC FUNCTIONS, in the order they appear: `is_leap_year`,
// `days_in_month`, `date_to_days`, `days_to_date`, `civil_date`, `civil_time`,
// `weekday_number`, `weekday_number_sunday_first`, `weekday`, `day_of_year`,
// `ordinal_to_date`, `iso_week_year`, `iso_week_number`, `iso_weekday` and
// `iso_week_to_date`. (Until cycle 0.1.3 the list ended at
// `weekday_number_sunday_first`, and this paragraph ended: "The derived fields
// -- the weekday of a date, ordinal dates, ISO week dates (C-13, C-14, C-15)
// -- are cycle 0.1.3's.")
//
// THE ALGORITHMS ARE HOWARD HINNANT'S, USED AS GIVEN (C-10, TM-016), AND THIS
// IS THEIR CITATION: Howard Hinnant, "chrono-Compatible Low-Level Date
// Algorithms", https://howardhinnant.github.io/date_algorithms.html -- the page
// dates itself 2021-09-01 and was read on 2026-09-25, per
// `meta/research/CURRENCY.md` (the digest is
// `meta/research/hinnant-date-algorithms.md`). `date_to_days` is his
// `days_from_civil` and `days_to_date` his `civil_from_days`, transcribed
// line for line and checked against the page, not against a copy of it. ONE
// DEVIATION: his intermediates are `unsigned` and these are `int64` (C-12),
// which makes the arithmetic total on inputs his `unsigned` would wrap.
//
// NO LOOP IN THIS MODULE. The first function here that loops adds
// `DecreasesViolated` to every consumer's bill (the compiler's D-304,
// `SAFETY.md` S-4b), which is eleven arms today -- a budget cost that would
// arrive with a `while` rather than with a decision, so it is written here.
//
// (Until cycle 0.1.1 this header's second paragraph read "NO ALGORITHM HERE.
// `date_to_days` and `days_to_date` ... are cycle 0.1.1's", and it ended:
// "What is here is the set of types those algorithms operate on, and the
// constructors that make an unreal date UNSPELLABLE.")
//
// UNSPELLABLE BY WHOM, precisely, because the answer changed at cycle 0.1.0c.
// Until then it was "by this library": a CONSUMER could write the struct
// literal and it compiled, linked and ran (C-8b, TM-148, measured at
// `aaffb87`). Since the fields are `sealed` (C-8c, TM-157) that literal is
// `NITPICK-TYPE-079` in every module but this one, measured at `c3bdae2`, so an
// unreal date is unspellable everywhere except here and through `wild` storage
// reinterpreted by `=>!` -- the language's opt-out for every checked property.
//
// `is_leap_year` AND `days_in_month` ARE HERE RATHER THAN AT 0.1.1, and the
// reason is recorded rather than discovered: `civil_date` must refuse February
// 30th (C-8), and a constructor that validates three of its four conditions is
// not a validating constructor. `0.1.0.md` §3 took that decision at planning.
//
// WHAT IT MAY IMPORT: `core` only (`BUILD.md` B-17). In particular NOT `zone` --
// that separation is what keeps a consumer doing plain calendar arithmetic at
// ONE identity arm rather than two (`SAFETY.md` S-4), and it is a budget
// decision, not a tidiness one.
//
// ===========================================================================
// NO FUNCTION IN THIS FILE RETURNS A SLICE OR A POINTER (S-18e)
// ===========================================================================
//
// Every gate this repository owns is a LEAK gate. D-151's exit-0 trap counts
// `wild` allocations and cannot see a managed body (TM-106); `check_raw_index`
// is about indexing; the undefined-symbol scan is blind to it (TM-118).
// **A use-after-free is a WRONG ANSWER, so it is found by a test that READS
// and by nothing else** -- and cycle 0.0 shipped two of them on this library's
// own public surface, `vec_pop<T>` (S-18d) and `bytes_view` (S-18e), both under
// a green suite, a review and an independent verification. (And a third,
// `bytes_take`'s answer, found at cycle 0.1.4b by the heap instrument's COUNT
// rather than by a test that reads -- S-18f.)
//
// This module is pure value arithmetic over `int64` and declares no owning
// type, so the class is much narrower here. The rule that keeps it narrow is
// the one above: if a function in this file ever seems to need to return a
// slice or a pointer, that is a STOP and a question, not a line of code.
//
// ===========================================================================
// THE DERIVES, AND THE PROBE VERDICT EACH ONE RESTS ON
// ===========================================================================
//
// `#[derive(Eq, Ord, Clone, Debug)]` on all five types.
//
//   `Ord` COMPARES IN DECLARATION ORDER -- the compiler's **D-123**, whose
//   table reads "`Ord`, `PartialOrd` | lexicographic comparison in declaration
//   order", and `tests/probe/probe01_derive_ord.npk` measured it on
//   `Timestamp`'s shape in both directions: `Pair{a,b}` and `Flip{b,a}`, same
//   fields, opposite verdicts. **CITE D-123 AND NOT D-051.** At every pin this
//   workbench has used, D-051 is "No `ostring`; portability lives in a `Path`
//   type above `nlibc`" -- a real heading, so nothing dangles and no reference
//   check can see the error (TM-143, and `src/core/vec.npk` carried exactly
//   this mis-citation for four subcycles).
//
//   `Eq` ON A PAYLOAD-FREE ENUM is not at issue in either direction.
//   `tests/probe/probe05b_derive_eq_refused.npk` measured `#[derive(Eq)]` on a
//   PAYLOAD enum: `NITPICK-TYPE-034` at the old pin, and FIXED at `aaffb87`
//   (TM-111). `Weekday` and `Month` carry no payload, so neither the old
//   behaviour nor the new one reaches them -- the probe is cited so a later
//   reader does not re-run it to find that out.
//
//   AND A DERIVE THAT COMPILES IS NOT A DERIVE THAT COMPARES CORRECTLY. That
//   is precisely what O-N10 was: `#[derive(Ord)]` on a payload enum compiled
//   and reported `Literal(7)` equal to `Literal(9)`. So the order is asserted
//   per FIELD by `tests/unit/civil_order.npk` rather than assumed from the
//   derive's presence.
//
// FIELD ORDER IS COMPARISON ORDER (C-6) AND IS NOT TO BE TIDIED. Year before
// month before day; hour before minute before second before nanos. The types
// below are copied from `CALENDAR.md` §3's code block VERBATIM -- that block is
// the authority (TM-002) and it is narrower than a declaration written from
// habit would be: the fields are NARROW, and the field is `nanos` and not
// `nano`.
mod:cal;

// The bounds this module checks against, by name. `SAFETY.md` S-12 makes every
// range stated and checked; S-16 and `check_constants_named` make the number
// itself live in `src/core/limits.npk` and nowhere else, with the rule that set
// it written beside it. A second literal copy here is how two modules come to
// disagree about a constant neither of them owns.
use "../core/limits.npk".NTIME_YEAR_MIN;
use "../core/limits.npk".NTIME_YEAR_MAX;
use "../core/limits.npk".NTIME_DAY_MIN;
use "../core/limits.npk".NTIME_DAY_MAX;
use "../core/limits.npk".NTIME_NANOS_PER_SEC;

// ===========================================================================
// The error identity -- the FIRST this library declares
// ===========================================================================

// `cal` DECLARES `ETimeValue` AND NOTHING ELSE (TM-017, S-1, S-2). Three is the
// whole library's budget and it is a CEILING: REACH-002 makes every identity a
// mandatory `failsafe` arm in every consuming program, so a fourth is a
// recorded decision AND a MAJOR version (TM-013), never a line of code.
//
// A DECLARATION ALONE ARMS NOTHING. The reachable set is computed from `fail`,
// `?!` and `!!!` SITES rather than from declarations -- measured at
// `tests/probe/probe11f_declared_unraised.npk` (S-4c). The `fail`s below are
// what put this name in every importing program's set.
pub error:ETimeValue;

// The caller's distinction, carried beside the identity rather than as a second
// identity (S-3): "out of range" and "not a real date" are one error to a
// `failsafe` and two different things to a caller.
//
// PAYLOAD-FREE, AND THE LANGUAGE LEAVES NO CHOICE: an `error:` CANNOT CARRY
// ONE. Measured at pin `aaffb87` --
// `tests/probe/probe14_error_payload_refused.npk` writes
// `pub error:ETimeValue(ValueFault);` and is refused `NITPICK-PARSE-001` at
// exit 1 with no `.ll` written, because `ErrorDecl` is `name` plus an optional
// explicit CODE (the prelude's alone) and has no payload slot at all. A
// `Result<T>` is `{ T value, tbb32 err }` and the error half is a code.
//
// SO C-5's "`ETimeValue` WITH A `ValueFault`" CANNOT MEAN AN ERROR PAYLOAD, and
// C-5b is the amendment that says what it does mean: the fault is a value the
// library computes, and the mechanism by which a refusing constructor hands it
// back is DELIBERATELY NOT DECIDED HERE (O-X8). Nothing in this cycle needs
// it, and adding a public name to settle it would be a TM-013 commitment taken
// by default. What IS settled is that the enum has one variant per refusal row
// of `0.1.0.md` §3, so the distinction exists to be delivered -- and, since
// cycle 0.1.3, one per refusal row of `ordinal_to_date` and `iso_week_to_date`
// too: a day of the year past the year's last (`DayOfYear`, beside
// `DayOfMonth`), a week below 1 (`WeekRange`) or past the week-year's last
// (`WeekOfYear`), and an ISO weekday outside 1 ... 7 (`WeekdayRange`). A fixed
// bound and a bound the year decides are two variants, as `DayRange` and
// `DayOfMonth` are (`SAFETY.md` S-3). Appended, so every earlier variant keeps
// its tag.
pub enum:ValueFault = {
    YearRange; MonthRange; DayRange; HourRange; MinuteRange;
    SecondRange; NanoRange; OffsetRange; DayOfMonth; Overflow;
    DayOfYear; WeekRange; WeekOfYear; WeekdayRange;
};

// ===========================================================================
// The types -- `CALENDAR.md` §3, verbatim
// ===========================================================================

// C-6: year, then month, then day -- so a derived `Ord` is exactly the ordering
// wanted and reordering the fields would silently change it.
//
// THE FIELDS ARE NARROW ON PURPOSE and the cost is one test, not a design.
// `#size_of<CivilDate>()` is 8 bytes rather than the 24 three `int64`s would
// take; `ZONE_MODEL.md`'s tables and `SPAN_MODEL.md`'s arithmetic both assume
// this layout. The cost is that every field is a NARROWING SITE and there is no
// checked narrowing (TM-105, S-15b) -- see `civil_date` below, where the
// validity check and the width check are the same check.
//
// EVERY FIELD IS `sealed`, SINCE CYCLE 0.1.0c (C-8c, TM-157; the compiler's
// D-313): read anywhere, WRITTEN ONLY IN THIS MODULE. Measured at `c3bdae2`, a
// consumer's `CivilDate{ year: 32000i32, month: 99u8, day: 99u8 }` is
// `NITPICK-TYPE-079` once per field it names, `a.month = 13u8` is `TYPE-079`,
// and a declared-uninitialised `CivilDate` cannot be read (`NITPICK-ASSIGN-001`,
// D-010). So outside `cal` the only way to hold a `CivilDate` is to have been
// handed one by `civil_date` -- or to reinterpret `wild` storage with `=>!`,
// the language's opt-out for every checked property. Reads, copies, `clone()`
// and the derived `cmp` all still work everywhere; `probe16e` pins that half.
// `sealed` and not `hidden`, because every field is READ across modules --
// `tests/unit/civil_order.npk` reads each one.
#[derive(Eq, Ord, Clone, Debug)]
pub struct:CivilDate = { sealed int32:year; sealed uint8:month; sealed uint8:day; };

// C-6 again: hour, minute, second, then nanos. The field is `nanos`, not
// `nano`, and it is `uint32` -- 999 999 999 fits, so the bound is SEMANTIC
// rather than a width check (C-9). Sealed field by field, like `CivilDate`
// above and for the same reason (C-8c, TM-157).
#[derive(Eq, Ord, Clone, Debug)]
pub struct:CivilTime = { sealed uint8:hour; sealed uint8:minute; sealed uint8:second; sealed uint32:nanos; };

// Date before time, so the derived `Ord` compares chronologically. Both members
// are named types, and a derived body reaches a named member through that
// member's own impl (the compiler's D-258) -- which is why both are declared
// above this one and both derive the same four.
//
// NOT SEALED, AND DELIBERATELY (TM-157). Its two members can only have come
// from `civil_date` and `civil_time`, so its literal checks nothing that was
// not already checked -- the reasoning of the "no `civil_datetime`" note below,
// unchanged. And the seals below it still hold THROUGH it: a write that reaches
// a sealed field through a path, `dt.date.month = 13u8`, is `TYPE-079` from
// outside `cal` like any other (measured at `c3bdae2`, cycle 0.1.0c's record).
#[derive(Eq, Ord, Clone, Debug)]
pub struct:CivilDateTime = { CivilDate:date; CivilTime:time; };

// C-7: MONDAY FIRST, matching ISO 8601. Declaration order is the tag order, so
// Monday is tag 0 and `weekday_number` below is that tag plus one.
//
// PAYLOAD-FREE IS DELIBERATE AND IS NOW LOAD-BEARING. O-N10's derives are fixed
// (TM-111) and a payload enum's `Ord` works at this pin, but every
// element-lifetime question disappears entirely for a payload-free one -- and
// the compiler's DERIVE-006 refuses four of the seven derives over an owning
// payload. `FORMAT_MODEL.md` §3's `Layout` vector is the only payload enum this
// library plans, and it is cycle 0.4's.
#[derive(Eq, Ord, Clone, Debug)]
pub enum:Weekday = { Monday; Tuesday; Wednesday; Thursday; Friday; Saturday; Sunday; };

// January first, so the tag is `month - 1` and indexes `MONTH_LENGTH` directly.
//
// NO `month_number` AND NO `month_from_number` IN THIS SUBCYCLE, and the
// absence is written down rather than left to look like an omission (TM-115).
// Neither the cycle README's 0.1.0 checklist nor `0.1.0.md` asks for one, this
// module's own arithmetic takes `int64:m` throughout, and every name on the
// public surface is a thing a MAJOR version is needed to take away (TM-013).
// The first consumer that needs the conversion is what should shape it.
#[derive(Eq, Ord, Clone, Debug)]
pub enum:Month   = { January; February; March; April; May; June;
                     July; August; September; October; November; December; };

// ===========================================================================
// The leap rule and the month lengths -- `CALENDAR.md` C-3
// ===========================================================================

// Days per month in a COMMON year, indexed by `month - 1`. February's 28 is the
// common-year value and `days_in_month` overrides it; the table holds the
// common year so that the leap correction is in exactly one place.
//
// A `fixed` TABLE IS `T[N]`, WHICH THE LANGUAGE BOUNDS-CHECKS (S-17b, second
// row): the emitter's `ExprIndexExpr` calls `emit_bounds_guard` for `TY_ARRAY`,
// so an out-of-range index here TRAPS rather than reading a wrong value. That
// is the opposite of `Vec<T>` and `Bytes`, whose bodies are bare pointers and
// whose accessors are the only bound that exists (TM-108). The index below is
// additionally checked before it is taken, so the guard is the belt.
fixed int64[12]:MONTH_LENGTH = [
    31i64, 28i64, 31i64, 30i64, 31i64, 30i64,
    31i64, 31i64, 30i64, 31i64, 30i64, 31i64
];

// C-3: divisible by 4 and not by 100, or divisible by 400. Applied UNIFORMLY
// across the whole supported range including negative years, because C-1 says
// proleptic.
//
// THE NEGATIVE-YEAR CORRECTION HINNANT'S ALGORITHM CARRIES IS NOT NEEDED HERE,
// AND THAT IS A MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN AN ARGUMENT. `0.1.0.md` §6 warned that
// `%` takes the sign of the DIVIDEND (C semantics, pinned by
// `tests/probe/probe07_negative_div.npk`) and that the leap rule is the first
// place it bites. It does not bite, for a reason worth stating because the
// warning was right about the general case: **every test here compares the
// remainder against ZERO, and zero has no sign.** `-4 % 4` is `0`, `-100 % 100`
// is `0`, `-400 % 400` is `0`. A sign correction would be needed the moment a
// NON-ZERO remainder were used -- as `days_from_civil`'s `era` does, which is
// why `days_from_civil` below carries `(y >= 0 ? y : y - 399) / 400` (0.1.1).
// (Until cycle 0.1.3 this sentence said `date_to_days` carries it, which was
// true until that cycle moved the formula into the private `days_from_civil`.)
//
// The four cases and their negative mirrors are named tests in
// `tests/unit/leap_rule.npk`; year -1, -4, -100 and -400 are there because a
// rule that is right by accident and a rule that is right pass the same
// positive-year suite.
pub func:is_leap_year = bool(int64:y) never fails {
    if ((y % 4i64) != 0i64)   { pass false; }
    if ((y % 100i64) != 0i64) { pass true; }
    pass ((y % 400i64) == 0i64);
};

// `requires m >= 1 && m <= 12`
// `ensures answer >= 28 && answer <= 31`
//
// (`VERIFICATION.md` P-1: contracts are comments in the syntax they will take
// at the compiler's 1.5. `answer`, never `result` -- `result` and `old` are
// reserved `VerificationKeyword`s, TM-130/B-18, and this library's own contract
// syntax is exactly where you meet them as things to write.)
//
// THE YEAR IS NOT RANGE-CHECKED HERE and that is deliberate: C-3's rule is
// defined for every `int64` year, the range is the CONSTRUCTOR's obligation
// (C-4), and a second copy of that check here would be a second place for the
// two to disagree. The month IS checked, because it indexes the table.
pub func:days_in_month = int64(int64:y, int64:m) {
    if (m < 1i64)  { fail ETimeValue; }
    if (m > 12i64) { fail ETimeValue; }
    if (m == 2i64) {
        if (raw is_leap_year(y)) { pass 29i64; }
        pass 28i64;
    }
    pass MONTH_LENGTH[m - 1i64];
};

// ===========================================================================
// The algorithms -- `CALENDAR.md` C-10 ... C-12 (TM-016), cycle 0.1.1
// ===========================================================================
//
// Howard Hinnant's `days_from_civil` and `civil_from_days`, TRANSCRIBED LINE
// FOR LINE from the page this module's header cites, with his own names --
// `era`, `yoe`, `mp`, `doy`, `doe` -- so the transcription can be checked
// against the page a line at a time rather than against a copy of it.
//
// THE ONE DEVIATION FROM THE PAGE: his intermediates are `unsigned` and these
// are `int64` (C-12). His `doy` is `[0, 365]` only for a real date, so on a
// `day` of 0 his `unsigned` wraps where this gives -1 -- which is what makes
// `date_to_days` TOTAL, and why the type is not to be "fixed" back.
//
// EVERY DIVISOR BELOW IS A POSITIVE INTEGER LITERAL (C-11): nonzero, so
// D-007's divide-by-zero trap is unreachable by construction, and not -1, so
// its `MIN / -1` overflow is too. `check_literal_divisors` fails the run on
// any other divisor anywhere in `src/cal/`, so that sentence is checked rather
// than remembered.
//
// NO LOOP. So no measure, and no `DecreasesViolated` in any consumer: `cal`'s
// bill stays 11 (`SAFETY.md` S-4). The first function here that loops adds
// that arm to every program importing this module.

// HINNANT'S `days_from_civil`, OVER ITS THREE INTEGERS -- the day number of
// `(y, m, d)`, epoch 1970-01-01 = 0. NOT PUBLIC. This is the body
// `date_to_days` held at cycles 0.1.1 and 0.1.2, moved here unchanged at
// cycle 0.1.3 (TM-170): the derived fields below need the day number of a date
// they do not hold -- 1 January of a date's year, 4 January of its week-year
// -- and building a `CivilDate` for it would put a `Result` inside functions
// that cannot fail. Hinnant's own function takes the three integers, so this
// is his signature again, and there is ONE copy of the formula in the module.
//
// TOTAL, AND ITS CALLERS KEEP C-12's BOUNDS. Every caller passes a month and
// day of 1 ... 12 and 1 ... 31 or a sealed field's, and a year within one of
// an `int32` -- a sealed field widened, or a range-checked year plus one -- so
// `|era| <= 5.4e6` and `|era * 146097| <= 7.9e11` hold of every call, nothing
// can overflow `int64`, and every divisor is a literal, so no input traps.
//
// `(y - 399) / 400` IS THE NEGATIVE-YEAR CORRECTION, NOT AN OFF-BY-ONE. `/`
// truncates toward zero (`tests/probe/probe07_negative_div.npk`), so for a
// negative `y` the plain quotient rounds the wrong way. The leap rule above
// needs no such correction and `era` does (`0.1.0.md` §6); vectors -1, -4,
// -100, -400 and -401 in `tests/unit/day_number_vectors.npk` are the
// correction under test.
//
// Hinnant's `y -= m <= 2;` adds a `bool` to an integer; here it is an `if`, on
// the parameter itself, as his page writes it.
func:days_from_civil = int64(int64:y, int64:m, int64:d) never fails {
    if (m <= 2i64) { y = y - 1i64; }
    int64:era = (is (y >= 0i64) : y : (y - 399i64)) / 400i64;
    int64:yoe = y - (era * 400i64);
    int64:mp = is (m > 2i64) : (m - 3i64) : (m + 9i64);
    int64:doy = (((153i64 * mp) + 2i64) / 5i64) + d - 1i64;
    int64:doe = (yoe * 365i64) + (yoe / 4i64) - (yoe / 100i64) + doy;
    pass (era * 146097i64) + doe - 719468i64;
};

// `ensures answer >= NTIME_DAY_MIN && answer <= NTIME_DAY_MAX`
//  (true of every `CivilDate` that `civil_date` can build -- C-8c)
//
// (`VERIFICATION.md` P-1 as `meta/OPEN_QUESTIONS.md` Q-6's recommended answer,
// A', reads it: a comment in the syntax it would take, `answer` for `result`
// (TM-130) -- and evidence of NOTHING, since a plain build checks no comment.
// The live `ensures` would arm `EnsuresViolated` in every consumer;
// `0.1.1.md` §6 is that alternative, measured.)
//
// THE DAY NUMBER OF A CIVIL DATE, epoch 1970-01-01 = 0. `never fails`, AND
// TOTAL OVER EVERY FIELD VALUE: with `year` an `int32` and `month` and `day`
// `uint8`, widened by the CHECKED `=>` (C-12), `|era| <= 5.4e6` and
// `|era * 146097| <= 7.9e11`, so nothing can overflow `int64`, and every
// divisor is a literal, so no input traps. After C-8c its argument can only
// have come from `civil_date`; the totality is C-8b's second bullet, kept
// because it costs nothing and `wild` storage reinterpreted by `=>!` is still
// an opt-out. A second copy of `civil_date`'s validity check here would be a
// second place for the two to disagree.
//
// ONE LINE SINCE CYCLE 0.1.3: the widening from the sealed fields, and
// `days_from_civil` above it is the algorithm (TM-170). Until then this
// function held the algorithm's nine lines itself.
pub func:date_to_days = int64(CivilDate:cd) never fails {
    pass raw days_from_civil(cd.year => int64, cd.month => int64, cd.day => int64);
};

// `ensures answer.month >= 1 && answer.month <= 12`
//  -- held by construction: it is `civil_date`'s result
// `prove(date_to_days(answer) == n)`      -- `VERIFICATION.md` §6's row
//
// THE CIVIL DATE OF A DAY NUMBER -- `civil_from_days`, with the same deviation.
//
// THE RANGE IS CHECKED FIRST, BEFORE `n + 719468` (S-12: checked before the
// trap). At `int64` MAX that addition would trap `IntOverflow`; a day outside
// `[NTIME_DAY_MIN, NTIME_DAY_MAX]` is `ETimeValue` instead, and
// `tests/unit/days_to_date_refused.npk` asserts the refusal arrives through
// the `Result` at both `int64` extremes, never through a trap. The fault is
// `YearRange` because the day lies outside C-4's years.
//
// IT BUILDS THROUGH `civil_date`, NOT A STRUCT LITERAL. So `civil_date` stays
// the only builder of a `CivilDate` in `src/` -- C-8 inside `cal`, the one
// module the seal does not reach (C-8c); the `int64` to `int32`/`uint8`
// narrowing is validate-then-narrow by construction (TM-105), with no `=>!`
// here; and an ALGORITHM error becomes a REFUSAL the 0.1.2 sweep sees rather
// than a wrong date it might not. For an `n` in range the `relay` fires only if
// the algorithm is wrong, and the sweep is what says it is not.
//
// Hinnant's `return (y + (m <= 2), m, d)` is the `if` before the `relay`.
pub func:days_to_date = CivilDate(int64:n) {
    if (n < NTIME_DAY_MIN) { fail ETimeValue; }   // ValueFault.YearRange
    if (n > NTIME_DAY_MAX) { fail ETimeValue; }   // ValueFault.YearRange
    int64:z = n + 719468i64;
    int64:era = (is (z >= 0i64) : z : (z - 146096i64)) / 146097i64;
    int64:doe = z - (era * 146097i64);
    int64:yoe = (doe - (doe / 1460i64) + (doe / 36524i64) - (doe / 146096i64)) / 365i64;
    int64:y = yoe + (era * 400i64);
    int64:doy = doe - ((365i64 * yoe) + (yoe / 4i64) - (yoe / 100i64));
    int64:mp = ((5i64 * doy) + 2i64) / 153i64;
    int64:d = doy - (((153i64 * mp) + 2i64) / 5i64) + 1i64;
    int64:m = is (mp < 10i64) : (mp + 3i64) : (mp - 9i64);
    if (m <= 2i64) { y = y + 1i64; }
    pass (relay civil_date(y, m, d));
};

// ===========================================================================
// The validating constructors -- C-8, and the whole contract of this cycle
// ===========================================================================

// `ensures answer.year == y && answer.month == m && answer.day == d`
//
// C-8: VALIDATING CONSTRUCTORS ONLY. There is no unchecked constructor in this
// module and there is not to be one. Everything downstream -- every conversion,
// every format, every zone lookup -- skips the question of whether a date is
// real BECAUSE `CivilDate` CANNOT HOLD AN UNREAL ONE. A single unchecked
// constructor added for convenience removes that guarantee everywhere at once,
// and it will be asked for.
//
// (THE LAST SENTENCE BUT ONE WAS FALSE OF A CONSUMER FROM CYCLE 0.1.0 TO
// 0.1.0b, and TM-148's count of the sites carrying that reading missed this
// one: at `aaffb87` a consumer's struct literal built month 99 at exit 0, and
// C-8b is what said so. SINCE CYCLE 0.1.0c IT IS TRUE, with exactly two
// exceptions, both stated: this module itself, which is why nothing in it
// builds a `CivilDate` but this function; and `wild` storage reinterpreted by
// `=>!`, the author's opt-out. Every field is `sealed`, so a consumer's literal
// or field write is `NITPICK-TYPE-079` -- C-8c, TM-157, measured at `c3bdae2`.
// Cycle 0.1.1's `date_to_days` is written total over every field value anyway,
// which costs nothing and survives the opt-out.)
//
// VALIDATE, THEN NARROW -- IN THAT ORDER, NEVER THE REVERSE. There is no
// checked narrowing in this language (TM-105, S-15b): `=>!` truncates in
// silence and the checked `=>` at the same narrowing is refused outright,
// `NITPICK-TYPE-009`. So every one of `CivilDate`'s three fields is a narrowing
// site and the range check before it is library code, written here.
//
// The order matters and the counter-example is a named test: a truncated value
// can land back INSIDE the valid range and pass the check that was supposed to
// catch it. `m = 269` truncates to `13` and would be refused by luck;
// **`m = 268` truncates to `12` and would be ACCEPTED, and wrong**. Validating
// first makes both refusals, and `tests/unit/civil_construct.npk` asserts the
// 268 case by name.
//
// The year bound is `int32`-narrowing as well as semantic, and is written as
// the SEMANTIC check against the named bound (S-13, TM-014: +/-9999, far inside
// `int32`). Written as a width check instead, a later widening of the supported
// range would silently become a truncation.
//
// TAKES `int64` ARGUMENTS, because anything narrower pushes the range problem
// to the caller -- which is the one place this library cannot check it.
pub func:civil_date = CivilDate(int64:y, int64:m, int64:d) {
    if (y < NTIME_YEAR_MIN) { fail ETimeValue; }   // ValueFault.YearRange
    if (y > NTIME_YEAR_MAX) { fail ETimeValue; }   // ValueFault.YearRange
    if (m < 1i64)           { fail ETimeValue; }   // ValueFault.MonthRange
    if (m > 12i64)          { fail ETimeValue; }   // ValueFault.MonthRange
    if (d < 1i64)           { fail ETimeValue; }   // ValueFault.DayRange
    // February 30th and April 31st. `relay` propagates `days_in_month`'s
    // refusal, which cannot fire here -- the month was checked three lines up
    // -- so this is the belt and not the answer.
    int64:dim = relay days_in_month(y, m);
    if (d > dim)            { fail ETimeValue; }   // ValueFault.DayOfMonth
    pass CivilDate{ year: y =>! int32, month: m =>! uint8, day: d =>! uint8 };
};

// `ensures answer.hour == h && answer.minute == mi`
// `ensures answer.second == s && answer.nanos == n`
//
// C-9 (TM-029): `hour in [0,23]`, `minute in [0,59]`, `second in [0,59]`,
// `nanos in [0, 999 999 999]`.
//
// HOUR 24 IS REFUSED. ISO 8601 permits `24:00:00` as an end-of-day marker and
// accepting it here would give midnight two spellings, which every comparison
// would then have to know about. The NORMALISING acceptance -- `24:00:00` read
// as `00:00:00` of the next day, with the fact recorded on the `Parsed` record
// -- belongs to the parser at cycle 0.4 (M-13, F-20) and is not smuggled in
// here.
//
// SECOND 60 IS REFUSED FOR THE SAME REASON. RFC 3339 permits it and the parser
// folds it to `:59` at 0.4; this type does not represent leap seconds
// (`TIME_MODEL.md`), so there is no value of `second` above 59 to construct.
//
// The nanosecond bound is `NTIME_NANOS_PER_SEC - 1i64` and not a literal: S-12
// makes every bound a named constant and `check_constants_named` fails the run
// on a second copy of 1000000000 outside its owner.
pub func:civil_time = CivilTime(int64:h, int64:mi, int64:s, int64:n) {
    if (h < 0i64)  { fail ETimeValue; }            // ValueFault.HourRange
    if (h > 23i64) { fail ETimeValue; }            // ValueFault.HourRange
    if (mi < 0i64) { fail ETimeValue; }            // ValueFault.MinuteRange
    if (mi > 59i64) { fail ETimeValue; }           // ValueFault.MinuteRange
    if (s < 0i64)  { fail ETimeValue; }            // ValueFault.SecondRange
    if (s > 59i64) { fail ETimeValue; }            // ValueFault.SecondRange
    if (n < 0i64)  { fail ETimeValue; }            // ValueFault.NanoRange
    if (n > (NTIME_NANOS_PER_SEC - 1i64)) { fail ETimeValue; }  // NanoRange
    pass CivilTime{ hour: h =>! uint8, minute: mi =>! uint8,
                    second: s =>! uint8, nanos: n =>! uint32 };
};

// NO `civil_datetime` CONSTRUCTOR, AND THE ABSENCE IS A DECISION (TM-115).
// `CivilDateTime` holds a `CivilDate` and a `CivilTime`, both of which can only
// have come from the two constructors above, so the struct literal
// `CivilDateTime{ date: d, time: t }` is exactly as validated as they are --
// there is no fourth condition for a constructor to check. A function that
// checked nothing would be an unchecked constructor on the surface of a module
// whose whole contract is C-8, which is the one shape this cycle is about not
// having.

// ===========================================================================
// Weekday numbering -- C-7
// ===========================================================================

// `ensures answer >= 1 && answer <= 7`
//
// ISO 8601: Monday = 1 ... Sunday = 7. Read off the TAG rather than written as
// a seven-arm `pick`, and the reason is that the enum's declaration order is
// already the statement of this fact (C-7): a `pick` would restate it a second
// time and the test a third, giving three copies of one rule that can drift
// apart. `tests/unit/civil_order.npk` asserts all seven values, so the tag
// mapping is checked rather than assumed.
//
// The tag is reached with `=>!` -- `=>` is refused on an enum -- and widened
// back with the CHECKED `=>`, which leaves exactly one unchecked cast in the
// function. A file where every cast is `=>!` hides which is which (S-15b).
pub func:weekday_number = int64(Weekday:w) never fails {
    pass ((w =>! int32) => int64) + 1i64;
};

// `ensures answer >= 0 && answer <= 6`
//
// C-7: Sunday = 0 ... Saturday = 6, the convention the C library uses. It is
// here because a caller porting code will look for it, and the enum's own order
// stays ISO's. Monday's tag 0 gives 1; Sunday's tag 6 gives 7 mod 7 = 0.
pub func:weekday_number_sunday_first = int64(Weekday:w) never fails {
    pass (((w =>! int32) => int64) + 1i64) % 7i64;
};

// ===========================================================================
// The derived fields -- `CALENDAR.md` C-13, C-14, C-15 (cycle 0.1.3, TM-169)
// ===========================================================================
//
// COMPUTED, NEVER STORED (C-13). Each is a function of the day number, so none
// can disagree with the date it describes: a stored weekday is a second
// representation of a fact the date already carries, and two representations
// drift.
//
// FIVE ARE `never fails` AND TOTAL; TWO BUILD A DATE BACK AND ARE VALIDATING
// CONSTRUCTORS, as `civil_date` and `days_to_date` are. Each constructor
// checks every argument against its bound BEFORE any arithmetic on it (S-12),
// and builds through `days_to_date`, so through `civil_date` -- which keeps
// `civil_date` the only builder of a `CivilDate` in `src/` (C-8).
//
// ONE WEEKDAY COMPUTATION, AND ONE CORRECTION. `weekday_index` is the only
// place this module reduces a day number modulo seven; `weekday`,
// `iso_weekday` and week 1's Monday all call it. `%` here takes the sign of
// the DIVIDEND (`tests/probe/probe07_negative_div.npk`), so `(n + 3) % 7` is
// negative on six days in seven before 1969-12-29, and the correction is what
// makes it a weekday at all (C-13).
//
// NO LOOP, SO NO MEASURE AND NO `DecreasesViolated`: `cal`'s bill stays 11,
// measured at planning with every function below in place. `#unreachable()`
// arms `Unreachable`, which is in the floor already.

// Monday = 0 ... Sunday = 6, the weekday of a DAY NUMBER (C-13): 1970-01-01 is
// a Thursday, index 3, so the index is `(n + 3) mod 7`, with the modulus made
// non-negative because `%` here truncates toward zero.
//
// `ensures answer >= 0 && answer <= 6` -- `VERIFICATION.md` §6's weekday row,
// a comment per Q-6's A' (TM-164) -- AND THE LINE THAT CHECKS IT IS CODE. The
// correction leaves `rem` in 0 ... 6 for every `int64` -- `%` by 7 lies in
// -6 ... 6, and the correction adds 7 to exactly the negative ones -- so the
// belt below never fires. It is there because `weekday` turns this index into
// a `Weekday` with `=>!`, which MANUFACTURES A TAG the compiler does not check
// (the compiler's D-140), and a tag outside 0 ... 6 is a `Weekday` that is
// none of the seven: every arm of a consumer's exhaustive `pick` misses it and
// the statement falls through (measured at planning, `0.1.3.md` §2). S-15b's
// rule -- a range check before every narrowing -- applied to a value this
// module computed: there is no caller to answer, so the failure is a
// controlled stop, not an `ETimeValue` (S-15c, TM-171).
func:weekday_index = int64(int64:n) never fails {
    int64:rem = (n + 3i64) % 7i64;
    if (rem < 0i64) { rem = rem + 7i64; }
    if ((rem < 0i64) || (rem > 6i64)) { #unreachable(); }
    pass rem;
};

// THE WEEKDAY OF A DATE (C-13, C-7), `Weekday.Monday` ... `Weekday.Sunday`.
// The index IS the tag, because the enum's declaration order is Monday first:
// `weekday_number` above reads the tag with `=>!` and this writes it with
// `=>!`, so C-7's order is stated once, by the enum, and not restated as a
// seven-arm `pick`. Both the `int64` to `int32` narrowing and the
// manufactured tag rest on `weekday_index`'s belt, on the same path (S-15b).
pub func:weekday = Weekday(CivilDate:cd) never fails {
    pass ((raw weekday_index(raw date_to_days(cd))) =>! int32) =>! Weekday;
};

// `ensures answer >= 1 && answer <= 366`
//
// THE DAY OF THE YEAR, 1 ... 366 (C-15): the day number less 1 January's, plus
// one -- from the day number, like every field here, and not from a table of
// month offsets, which would be a second statement of the month lengths
// beside `MONTH_LENGTH`.
pub func:day_of_year = int64(CivilDate:cd) never fails {
    pass ((raw date_to_days(cd)) - (raw days_from_civil(cd.year => int64, 1i64, 1i64))) + 1i64;
};

// `ensures day_of_year(answer) == doy`
//
// THE DATE OF AN ORDINAL DATE -- the round trip's other half (C-15,
// `TESTING.md` V-4). A validating constructor: the year against C-4's range,
// the day against 1 and the year's length, each BEFORE any arithmetic on it
// (S-12), so neither `y + 1` nor `first + doy` can overflow, and
// `tests/unit/ordinal_to_date_refused.npk` asserts the refusal arrives through
// the `Result` at both `int64` extremes of both arguments. The year's length
// is the distance between two consecutive 1 Januaries -- Hinnant's answer,
// not `is_leap_year`'s -- because the sweep that checks this function counts
// the days with the leap rule and the month table, and the two sides then
// share no code.
pub func:ordinal_to_date = CivilDate(int64:y, int64:doy) {
    if (y < NTIME_YEAR_MIN) { fail ETimeValue; }   // ValueFault.YearRange
    if (y > NTIME_YEAR_MAX) { fail ETimeValue; }   // ValueFault.YearRange
    if (doy < 1i64)         { fail ETimeValue; }   // ValueFault.DayRange
    int64:first = raw days_from_civil(y, 1i64, 1i64);
    int64:len = (raw days_from_civil(y + 1i64, 1i64, 1i64)) - first;
    if (doy > len)          { fail ETimeValue; }   // ValueFault.DayOfYear
    pass (relay days_to_date((first + doy) - 1i64));
};

// THE DAY NUMBER OF THE MONDAY THAT BEGINS WEEK 1 OF WEEK-YEAR `iy` (C-14).
// ISO 8601's week 1 is the week that holds the year's first Thursday, which is
// the week that holds 4 January -- the first Thursday is one of 1 ... 7
// January, and the Monday-to-Sunday week around it holds the 4th exactly then
// -- so its Monday is 4 January less 4 January's weekday index. EVERY ISO
// FIELD IN THIS MODULE IS READ OFF THIS ONE FUNCTION, forwards and back, so
// the two directions cannot disagree about where a week-year begins; the sweep
// that checks them reads the rule the other way, from the month and day of
// each Monday (TM-172, `tests/unit/sweep/every_iso_week_date.npk`).
func:iso_week_one = int64(int64:iy) never fails {
    int64:jan4 = raw days_from_civil(iy, 1i64, 4i64);
    pass jan4 - (raw weekday_index(jan4));
};

// `ensures answer >= cd.year - 1 && answer <= cd.year + 1` --
// `VERIFICATION.md` §6's "the week-year is within one of the calendar year"
//
// THE WEEK-YEAR OF A DATE (C-14): the year its ISO week belongs to, which is a
// neighbour of the calendar year for up to three days at either end -- 1
// January 2021 is in week 53 of 2020, and 31 December 2012 in week 1 of 2013.
pub func:iso_week_year = int64(CivilDate:cd) never fails {
    int64:n = raw date_to_days(cd);
    int64:y = cd.year => int64;
    if (n < (raw iso_week_one(y)))          { pass y - 1i64; }
    if (n >= (raw iso_week_one(y + 1i64)))  { pass y + 1i64; }
    pass y;
};

// `ensures answer >= 1 && answer <= 53` -- `VERIFICATION.md` §6's ISO row
//
// THE WEEK NUMBER, 1 ... 53 (C-14): whole weeks since the week-year's first
// Monday, plus one. The difference is 0 ... 370, never negative, so `/`
// truncating toward zero is the floor it needs to be.
pub func:iso_week_number = int64(CivilDate:cd) never fails {
    int64:n = raw date_to_days(cd);
    pass ((n - (raw iso_week_one(raw iso_week_year(cd)))) / 7i64) + 1i64;
};

// `ensures answer >= 1 && answer <= 7`
//
// THE ISO WEEKDAY, Monday = 1 ... Sunday = 7 (C-14, C-7): what
// `weekday_number(weekday(cd))` gives, from the same `weekday_index` and
// without the enum between them.
pub func:iso_weekday = int64(CivilDate:cd) never fails {
    pass (raw weekday_index(raw date_to_days(cd))) + 1i64;
};

// `ensures iso_week_year(answer) == iy && iso_week_number(answer) == w`
// `ensures iso_weekday(answer) == wd`
//
// THE DATE OF AN ISO WEEK DATE -- the round trip's other half (C-14,
// `TESTING.md` V-4). A validating constructor, each argument checked BEFORE
// the arithmetic that uses it (S-12): the week-year against C-4's range, the
// weekday against 1 ... 7, the week against 1 and against the week-year's own
// count -- 52, or 53 in a long year -- which is the distance between two
// consecutive week-1 Mondays. WEEK 53 OF A 52-WEEK YEAR IS REFUSED, not read
// as week 1 of the next: one date, one spelling.
//
// TWO DAYS OF THE LAST WEEK-YEAR LIE OUTSIDE THE RANGE. 9999's week 52 runs to
// Sunday 10000-01-02, so `(9999, 52, 6)` and `(9999, 52, 7)` are refused by
// `days_to_date`'s range check and relayed -- `ValueFault.YearRange`, the day
// being outside C-4's years. The first week-year has no such edge:
// -9999-01-01 is a Monday, so it begins week 1 of -9999.
pub func:iso_week_to_date = CivilDate(int64:iy, int64:w, int64:wd) {
    if (iy < NTIME_YEAR_MIN) { fail ETimeValue; }   // ValueFault.YearRange
    if (iy > NTIME_YEAR_MAX) { fail ETimeValue; }   // ValueFault.YearRange
    if (w < 1i64)            { fail ETimeValue; }   // ValueFault.WeekRange
    if (wd < 1i64)           { fail ETimeValue; }   // ValueFault.WeekdayRange
    if (wd > 7i64)           { fail ETimeValue; }   // ValueFault.WeekdayRange
    int64:first = raw iso_week_one(iy);
    int64:weeks = ((raw iso_week_one(iy + 1i64)) - first) / 7i64;
    if (w > weeks)           { fail ETimeValue; }   // ValueFault.WeekOfYear
    pass (relay days_to_date(first + ((w - 1i64) * 7i64) + (wd - 1i64)));
};
